# Agent Instructions

This project is a .NET extractor integration built on the Intropy framework.
One run = one sweep of the inbound folder; each swept file goes through the
Intropy extractor pipeline and is published as a CloudEvent to a Dapr pub/sub
topic, then the source file is deleted. Scheduling lives outside the block —
activation cadence is deployment configuration (a Kubernetes CronJob in
production); locally the system host runs the block once at startup.

## Project overview

- Framework: .NET 10
- App type: Run-to-completion console job (no web endpoints; one sweep per run,
  then exit). Hosted by the framework's `RunToCompletionRunner`, which owns the
  Dapr sidecar lifecycle, tracing, and the exit-code contract: `0` success /
  no-op / cancelled, `1` job failure (a file failed and was left in place), `2`
  infrastructure failure (sidecar unavailable).
- Runner: the framework's `RunToCompletionRunner` (in `Intropy.Framework.Hosting`),
  driven by `{{ .name }}.ExtractJob` — an `IRunToCompletionJob` that adapts
  `{{ .name }}.Sweep` (the sidecar-free one-shot sweep) and reports a
  `JobRunSummary`.
- App id: `{{ .appId }}`
- Source port: `{{ .port }}` — the named interface the extractor reads
  the outside world through; the system host declares it in `Ports.cs`
  and the Dapr binding uses the same name, resolved to a local folder in
  development. In production the same name resolves to the environment's
  binding (e.g. SFTP) from the deploy overlay's GitOps values — rebinding
  the port is a deployment change, never a code change
- Pub/sub component: `{{ .pubsub }}`
- Topic: `{{ .topicName }}` — the extractor's half of the system contract; the
  consuming loader subscribes to the same topic
{{- if .messageId }}
- Message: `{{ .messageId }}` — what this extractor publishes, by name. It is
  the CloudEvent type, and the name a consuming loader subscribes by
{{- if .schemaUrl }}
- Payload schema: `{{ .schemaUrl }}` — the schema version pinned when the
  message was resolved, and the input the contract type is generated from.
  The schema document itself is committed under `../{{ .contracts }}/schemas/`;
  `../{{ .contracts }}/README.md` carries the command that generates
  `{{ .contract }}` from it
{{- end }}
{{- end }}
- CloudEvent envelope: source `{{ if .eventSource }}{{ .eventSource }}{{ else }}{{ .eventSourceValue }}{{ end }}`, type `{{ if .messageId }}{{ .messageId }}{{ else if .eventType }}{{ .eventType }}{{ else }}{{ .eventTypeValue }}{{ end }}`
- Idempotency Service: reached via Dapr service invocation, app id
  `{{ .idempotencyAppId }}` (from `src/appsettings.json`, overridable per environment)
- Business Incident Service: reached via Dapr service invocation, app id
  `{{ .businessIncidentsAppId }}` (from `src/appsettings.json`, overridable per environment)
- Shared contracts: the published contract (`{{ .contract }}`, `OrderLine`) lives in the
  sibling project `../{{ .contracts }}` (namespace `{{ .contracts }}`), referenced
  from `src/{{ .name }}.csproj`. The sibling is scaffolded by this template's
  dependency declaration; when it already exists (another component created
  it) the scaffold leaves it untouched. `Source{{ .contract }}` (the source file shape) stays
  local in `src/Model/`.

## Important files

- `Taskfile.yml` - build and test tasks (`task build`, `task test`; the
  suites also run separately via `task test:unit` / `task test:integration`).
- `Dockerfile` - multi-stage image build on the .NET chiseled runtime
  (`docker build --build-context contracts=../{{ .contracts }} -t {{ .appId }} .` —
  the shared contracts sibling enters as a named context); the image contains
  only the app — the Dapr sidecar and components come from the deployment
  environment.
- `src/Program.cs` - entrypoint: builds the service provider via `Composition`
  and runs the framework `RunToCompletionRunner`.
- `src/Composition/Composition.cs` - every service registration (including
  the pipeline and the publish edge: the sender is a DI-registered
  `SendStep<Context>` — a `DaprTopicPublisher` in production — resolved by
  `BuildPipeline(provider)` like any other external). The idempotency key
  extractors (`OrderId`/`OrderDate`) are inlined in `BuildPipeline`. Note the
  namespace/type collision: `{{ .name }}.Composition` is both namespace and
  class, so call sites inside nested namespaces use the fully qualified
  `Composition.Composition.BuildPipeline(...)`.
- `src/ExtractJob.cs` - adapts `Sweep` to the framework's `IRunToCompletionJob`;
  a thin pass-through returning the sweep's `JobRunSummary`
  (Processed/Failed/Cancelled). `Failed > 0` drives the job-failure exit code;
  a delete-failure-after-publish counts as `Failed` (the file stays).
- `src/Sweep.cs` - the one-shot sweep: list inbound, run the pipeline per
  file, delete on success. Sidecar-free on purpose: the integration suite
  constructs it directly against the framework's testing fakes.
- `src/Constants.cs` - component/topic names, keyed-adapter key, CloudEvent
  envelope values (root namespace — consumed by both `src` and the tests).
- `src/Process/` - the extractor pipeline steps only (`Deserializer`,
  `Validator`, `Transformer`); serialization is the framework's
  `CloudEventSerializeStep`. All DI and `ExtractorBuilder` wiring lives in
  `src/Composition/`.
- `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Unit/` - pipeline step unit tests (pure xUnit; no
  fakes, no Dapr; `task test:unit`).
- `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Integration/` - sweep, publish-wiring, composition,
  and runner tests against `Intropy.Framework.Testing` fakes
  (`task test:integration`; `task coverage` reports branch coverage).

## Configuration

Settings live in `src/appsettings.json` (copied to the build output), overridden by
`appsettings.{Environment}.json` and environment variables — `Composition` builds
`IConfiguration` from the binary directory, registers it in DI, and reads the
platform-service app ids from `PlatformServices:*`. Deployment overlays the file
via ConfigMap. Components that need typed options bind them themselves.

## Build and test

Components do not run standalone — this extractor runs via its system host,
which runs it once at startup and provides every Dapr component (source
binding, pub/sub, platform services). A re-run is a host restart. The
component-level loop is build and test:

```bash
task build
task test
```

`task test` runs both test projects (`task test:unit` / `task test:integration`
run them separately): the unit suite pins each pipeline step's
own contract; the integration suite builds the pipeline exactly as production
composition does, fakes only the edges (`InMemoryFileAdapter`, `FakeTopic`,
`FakeIdempotencyServiceClient`, `FakeBusinessIncidentServiceClient` — from
`Intropy.Framework.Testing`), and asserts on fake state. NSubstitute appears
only for the `DaprClient` publish seam the testing library deliberately
leaves open (`PublishIntegrationTests`). `task coverage` runs the integration suite with branch
coverage (Cobertura report under the integration project's `TestResults/`).

## Development notes

- The source `IFileAdapter` is registered as a keyed singleton
  (`Constants.SourceFileAdapterKey`). Inject it via `[FromKeyedServices(...)]`
  or resolve via `GetRequiredKeyedService<IFileAdapter>(...)`.
- A failed file stays in the inbound folder and is retried on the next
  scheduled run; the idempotency check makes the retry safe. Duplicates cancel
  as a traceable no-op and the file is removed.
- Additional enrichment steps go into the builder chain in
  `src/Composition/Composition.cs` (`BuildPipeline`) via `.WithExtractor(...)` —
  the extractor pipeline supports any number of them, executed in order.
{{- if .empty }}
- The step bodies are scaffolded empty (`NotImplementedException`). The
  idempotency and business-incident wiring stays in place — `ExtractorBuilder`
  requires both — but the idempotency extractor lambdas throw. When you
  implement the steps, point them at a stable business identifier and business
  timestamp; a per-delivery value (fresh GUID, wall-clock time) makes the
  check pass everything.
- The test suite is real and fails out of the box by design — `task test` is
  red until the steps and the extractor lambdas land (only `CompositionTests`
  starts green). The pinned behaviors flip green one by one as the
  implementation progresses; no test edits are needed to get there. Three
  sweep tests from the full sample suite are deliberately not scaffolded:
  business-failure incident routing (which input fails validation is the
  Validator's to define; scaffolded now its assertions would hold vacuously),
  duplicate-cancel, and delete-failure-redelivery (both need the duplicate
  simulation the idempotency extractors enable). Add them as the
  corresponding implementation lands, copying the full sample suite's
  versions.
{{- else }}
- The pipeline checks idempotency before transforming: the id is the order's
  `OrderId` and the timestamp its `OrderDate`, so a redelivered unchanged order
  cancels as a traceable no-op instead of publishing twice. Keep the id/date
  extractors pointing at *business* fields — a per-delivery value (fresh GUID,
  wall-clock time) makes the check pass everything.
- Business failures (validation errors) are routed to the Business Incident
  Service, keyed on the order id (falling back to the source file name).
- The framework's `CloudEventSerializeStep` builds the CloudEvent envelope and
  validates `Subject`/`Time` at serialize time (the publisher keeps its checks
  as defense-in-depth). The component supplies both as lambdas in `BuildPipeline`.
{{- end }}

## Framework guidance

How-to knowledge for the Intropy framework (pipelines, blocks, endpoints, Dapr components, testing) lives in the Intropy skills collection under `.agents/skills/`. If it is not installed, run `intropy skills collection add --name intropy --ref harbor.intropy.io/skills/index:latest`.
